Validated — `runnable: true`. The pipeline produces `diar_segments` (who-spoke-when) with clean per-segment timestamps, and the only notices are informational (`ManifestReader` is a composite; Sortformer downloads its model on first run). Here's the runnable recipe:

```recipe
stages:
  - {ref: ManifestReader, params: {manifest_path: REQUIRED}}
  - {ref: ResampleAudioStage, params: {resampled_audio_dir: REQUIRED, target_sample_rate: 16000, target_nchannels: 1, update_audio_filepath: true}}
  - {ref: InferenceSortformerStage, params: {}}
  - {ref: TimestampMapperStage, params: {}}
  - {ref: ManifestWriterStage, params: {output_path: REQUIRED}}
```

**Rationale:** Your goal is speaker diarization, so the core module is `InferenceSortformerStage` (NVIDIA Sortformer) — chosen over `PyAnnoteDiarizationStage` because you confirmed ≤4 English speakers and wanted no extra account setup (PyAnnote is gated behind a HuggingFace token); Sortformer's 4-speaker cap and English/conversational domain fit exactly. Sortformer only accepts 16 kHz, so `ResampleAudioStage` converts your 48 kHz files to 16 kHz mono once upstream (`update_audio_filepath: true` repoints the audio at the resampled copy so the model reads the right file — the card fact that mattered here); `TimestampMapperStage` normalizes the who-spoke-when segments into a clean `original_file`/start/end/speaker schema, and `ManifestWriterStage` writes the JSONL. There's no quality threshold to `resolve` for diarization (it has no MOS/WER anchor). One assumption: I treated these as single-file-per-meeting recordings needing whole-file diarization (no pre-segmentation). To proceed, fill in the three `REQUIRED` paths and I'll run a bounded 10-file smoke test on a GPU, show you retained/rejected evidence and the scale/time estimate, and only run at full scale after you confirm.
